 <h2 id="dl-troubleshoot-headline" class="section-headline">
 Common problems and solutions.
 </h2>
 </div>

 <div class="dl-usecases-grid">
 <article class="dl-usecase-card">
 <h3 class="dl-usecase-title">"The Reel won't download."</h3>
 <p class="dl-usecase-body">
 Nine times out of ten, the link is wrong. Someone copied a link from a comment instead of the actual Reel, or the URL got mangled during copy-paste. Go back to Instagram, open the Reel itself, tap Share → Copy Link, and paste that fresh URL. If the Reel was deleted by the creator, no downloader can retrieve it -- not TikSaveHub, not anyone else.
 </p>
 </article>

 <article class="dl-usecase-card">
 <h3 class="dl-usecase-title">"The quality is low."</h3>
 <p class="dl-usecase-body">
 Quality depends on what the creator uploaded. Most Reels are filmed on phones at 1080p, but some creators upload at 720p or lower. TikSaveHub fetches whatever resolution Instagram has -- it can't improve what was already compressed. If the Reel looks blurry on Instagram, it'll look the same downloaded. That's a source-quality issue, not a tool limitation.
 </p>
 </article>

 <article class="dl-usecase-card">
 <h3 class="dl-usecase-title">"The link isn't working."</h3>
 <p class="dl-usecase-body">
 TikSaveHub handles all standard Instagram Reel link formats -- instagram.com/reel/..., instagram.com/reels/..., instagram.com/share/..., and the shortened links from the app. Profile pages, hashtag pages, and search results aren't downloadable -- they don't point to a specific Reel. The link needs to contain /reel/, /reels/, or /share/.
 </p>
 </article>

 <article class="dl-usecase-card">
 <h3 class="dl-usecase-title">"Download takes too long."</h3>
 <p class="dl-usecase-body">
 The download itself is fast -- under three seconds. The bottleneck is file transfer. A typical Reel is 5 to 15MB as MP4. On a 10 Mbps connection, that's under twelve seconds of download time. On slower mobile data, it takes longer. Switch to Wi-Fi if it's dragging.
 </p>
 </article>

 <article class="dl-usecase-card">
 <h3 class="dl-usecase-title">"The watermark is still there."</h3>
 <p class="dl-usecase-body">
 That shouldn't happen with TikSaveHub. If you're seeing a watermark, you might be using a different tool -- some downloaders add their own branding. TikSaveHub fetches the original file from Instagram's CDN with no overlay. Try clearing your browser cache and downloading again.
 </p>
